var documenterSearchIndex = {"docs":
[{"category":"section","location":"tutorials/schur_reduction/#Schur-Complement-Reduction","page":"Schur Complement Reduction","text":"Bundle adjustment and similar problems are bipartite: a few retained variable blocks (cameras, B) are coupled to many small eliminated blocks (landmarks, C) through coupling blocks E. The normal equations\n\nbeginbmatrix B  E  E^top  C endbmatrix\nbeginbmatrix x_c  x_p endbmatrix =\nbeginbmatrix b_c  b_p endbmatrix\n\nare solved cheaply by eliminating C, which is block diagonal, then solving the much smaller reduced system S x_c = g with S = B - E C^-1 E^top and g = b_c - E C^-1 b_p, then recovering x_p = C^-1(b_p - E^top x_c). BlockLinearSystems provides the data structures and the parallel kernels for this; the optimizer-facing linear systems built on them live in SecondOrderOptimization.jl.","title":"Schur Complement Reduction"},{"category":"section","location":"tutorials/schur_reduction/#Describing-the-problem","page":"Schur Complement Reduction","text":"A SchurStructure records the block dimensions and which retained blocks observe each eliminated block. Eliminated blocks may have any dimension from 1 to 255: up to BlockLinearSystems.D_STATIC (8) they use statically sized register arithmetic, larger ones use a LAPACK Cholesky on a scratch matrix. The numerical values live in a SchurBlockMatrix: B (a dense Matrix or a SparseBlockLinearSystem), C_data (the eliminated diagonal blocks, each stored column-major) and E_data (one dp × dc block per coupling, stored as the transpose of the retained-by-eliminated block).","title":"Describing the problem"},{"category":"section","location":"tutorials/schur_reduction/#A-complete-example","page":"Schur Complement Reduction","text":"using BlockLinearSystems, LinearAlgebra, Random\n\nrng = Random.Xoshiro(1)\nc_dims = [6, 6, 6]                 # three cameras\np_dims = [3, 3, 3, 3, 2, 1]        # six landmarks of dimension 3, 3, 3, 3, 2, 1\nobservations = [[1, 2], [1, 2, 3], [2, 3], [1, 3], [1, 2], [3]]\nst = SchurStructure(c_dims, p_dims, observations)\n\n# A random diagonally dominant (so positive definite) Hessian with exactly the structure described above:\n# dense B, block-diagonal C, and a coupling block wherever a camera observes a landmark\nn = st.nc + sum(p_dims)\nH = zeros(n, n)\nH[1:st.nc, 1:st.nc] .= rand(rng, st.nc, st.nc)\nfor l in 1:st.npblocks\n    p0, dp = st.nc + st.boffsets_p[l], p_dims[l]\n    H[p0:p0+dp-1, p0:p0+dp-1] .= rand(rng, dp, dp)\n    for e in st.cpptr[l]:st.cpptr[l+1]-1\n        c0, dc = st.boffsets_c[st.cpblock[e]], c_dims[st.cpblock[e]]\n        H[c0:c0+dc-1, p0:p0+dp-1] .= rand(rng, dc, dp)\n    end\nend\nH = H + H'\nH[diagind(H)] .= sum(abs, H; dims=2)[:] .+ 1\nb = randn(rng, n)\n\n# Fill B, C and E (E is stored transposed: E_data[eo + (r-1)*dp + i - 1] = H[camera row r, landmark i])\nB = H[1:st.nc, 1:st.nc]\nC_data = zeros(sum(abs2, p_dims))\nE_data = zeros(sum(c_dims[st.cpblock[e]] * p_dims[l] for l in 1:st.npblocks for e in st.cpptr[l]:st.cpptr[l+1]-1))\nfor l in 1:st.npblocks\n    dp, po, p0 = p_dims[l], st.ppoffsets[l], st.nc + st.boffsets_p[l]\n    C_data[po:po+dp^2-1] .= vec(H[p0:p0+dp-1, p0:p0+dp-1])\n    for e in st.cpptr[l]:st.cpptr[l+1]-1\n        c = st.cpblock[e]; dc = c_dims[c]; c0 = st.boffsets_c[c]\n        E_data[st.cpoffsets[e]:st.cpoffsets[e]+dc*dp-1] .= vec(H[c0:c0+dc-1, p0:p0+dp-1]')\n    end\nend\nHs = SchurBlockMatrix(st, B, C_data, E_data)\n\nReduce, solve the small system, and back-substitute. lambda and scaling add Levenberg–Marquardt damping lambda * diag(scaling) to the whole system (the scaling is the damping diagonal itself, e.g. abs.(diag(H)) for Marquardt scaling):\n\nlambda, scaling = 0.1, ones(n)\nnc = st.nc\nws = SchurReductionWorkspace{Float64}(st, 1)      # one chunk; use more for multithreading\nS = zeros(nc, nc); g = zeros(nc)\nreset_schur_dense!(S, Hs.B, view(scaling, 1:nc), lambda, ws)\nreduce_schur_dense!(S, g, view(b, 1:nc), view(b, nc+1:n), view(scaling, nc+1:n), lambda, st, C_data, E_data, ws) ||\n    error(\"an eliminated block is singular\")\nx = zeros(n)\nx[1:nc] .= Symmetric(S, :U) \\ g                   # only the upper triangle of S is filled\nbacksubstitute!(view(x, nc+1:n), view(x, 1:nc), st, E_data, ws.C_inv_data, ws.z_data)\n\nx_full = (H + lambda * Diagonal(scaling)) \\ b     # reference: solve the whole system\nnorm(x - x_full) / norm(x_full)\n4.462260659512839e-16\n\nThe reduction returns false if an eliminated block is numerically singular (or not positive definite), and ws.failed_landmark[] then holds the lowest failing block index.","title":"A complete example"},{"category":"section","location":"tutorials/schur_reduction/#Sparse-reduced-systems","page":"Schur Complement Reduction","text":"When many camera pairs share no landmark, S is sparse. build_schur_covisibility computes its pattern, and build_b_to_s_map maps the stored blocks of a SparseBlockLinearSystem B to it; reduce_schur_sparse! then fills S.nzval directly. Retained–retained couplings that go through no eliminated block (odometry or loop-closure factors) need slots too: pass them as extra_blocks, which retained_block_pairs extracts from B:\n\ncovis = build_schur_covisibility(st; extra_blocks=retained_block_pairs(B))\nS = covis.S_sparse\nb_to_s = build_b_to_s_map(B, covis, st.boffsets_c)\nreset_schur_sparse!(S.nzval, B.data, b_to_s, covis.diag_inds, scaling_c, lambda, ws)\nreduce_schur_sparse!(S, g, b_c, b_p, scaling_p, lambda, st, C_data, E_data,\n                     covis.block_colptr, covis.block_rowval, covis.block_row_offsets, covis.col_heights, ws)","title":"Sparse reduced systems"},{"category":"section","location":"tutorials/schur_reduction/#How-the-reduction-is-parallelised","page":"Schur Complement Reduction","text":"The reduction runs in two phases. Phase 1 factors every eliminated block (in parallel over chunks of eliminated blocks) and stores C⁻¹, z = C⁻¹ g_p and W = C⁻¹Eᵀ. Phase 2 splits the retained blocks into contiguous ranges (ws.col_chunks); each chunk writes only its own columns of the single S, so there are no per-chunk copies of S and no merge pass. Loops run serially unless more than one thread is available and there is enough work (BlockLinearSystems.SCHUR_THREAD_MIN_WORK); threading uses Polyester.","title":"How the reduction is parallelised"},{"category":"section","location":"tutorials/schur_reduction/#Trial-systems","page":"Schur Complement Reduction","text":"Optimizers that linearize at a trial point keep the current system for retries. copy_structure(::SchurBlockMatrix) allocates new zeroed B, C_data and E_data sharing only the immutable structure, and trial_workspace gives a workspace with its own caches.","title":"Trial systems"},{"category":"section","location":"tutorials/schur_reduction/#Using-it-with-SecondOrderOptimization.jl","page":"Schur Complement Reduction","text":"Loading both BlockLinearSystems and SecondOrderOptimization activates an extension that wraps all of this in DenseSchurLinearSystem and SparseSchurLinearSystem (selected with linsolver = :schur, :dense_schur or :sparse_schur), for use with its Levenberg–Marquardt, dogleg and Newton iterators.","title":"Using it with SecondOrderOptimization.jl"},{"category":"section","location":"api/#API-Reference","page":"API Reference","text":"","title":"API Reference"},{"category":"section","location":"api/#Linear-System-Types","page":"API Reference","text":"","title":"Linear System Types"},{"category":"section","location":"api/#Block-Indexing-and-Accessors","page":"API Reference","text":"","title":"Block Indexing & Accessors"},{"category":"section","location":"api/#Conversion-and-Symmetrification","page":"API Reference","text":"","title":"Conversion & Symmetrification"},{"category":"section","location":"api/#Sparsity-Statistics","page":"API Reference","text":"","title":"Sparsity Statistics"},{"category":"section","location":"api/#Run-Length-Encoded-Block-Arrangements","page":"API Reference","text":"","title":"Run-Length Encoded Block Arrangements"},{"category":"section","location":"api/#Schur-Complement-Reduction","page":"API Reference","text":"Data structures and kernels for bipartite problems, where retained variables (e.g. cameras) are coupled through eliminated variables (e.g. landmarks). See the Schur reduction tutorial.","title":"Schur Complement Reduction"},{"category":"section","location":"api/#Reduced-System-Sparsity","page":"API Reference","text":"","title":"Reduced-System Sparsity"},{"category":"section","location":"api/#Variable-Ordering","page":"API Reference","text":"","title":"Variable Ordering"},{"category":"section","location":"api/#Internal-Utilities","page":"API Reference","text":"","title":"Internal Utilities"},{"category":"section","location":"api/#Schur-Reduction-Kernels","page":"API Reference","text":"","title":"Schur Reduction Kernels"},{"category":"type","location":"api/#BlockLinearSystems.SparseBlockLinearSystem","page":"API Reference","text":"SparseBlockLinearSystem{T}(sparsitytransposed::SparseMatrixCSC{Bool, Int}, rowblocksizes, colblocksizes=rowblocksizes)\nSparseBlockLinearSystem{T}(nnzvals, indicestransposed, rowblocksizes, colblocksizes=rowblocksizes)\n\nCache-friendly block-sparse matrix storing each non-zero block contiguously in column-major order within a flat data::Vector{T} buffer.\n\nThe block sparsity pattern is provided transposed (sparsitytransposed has size (length(colblocksizes), length(rowblocksizes))) so that blocks along a block-row are contiguous in the CSC structure of indicestransposed.\n\nFields\n\ndata::Vector{T}: Contiguous storage for all non-zero block entries.\nindices::SparseMatrixCSC{Int, Int}: Cached start indices in data for each block (row, col).\nindicestransposed::SparseMatrixCSC{Int, Int}: Transposed start indices in data (indicestransposed[col, row]).\nrowblocksizes::Vector{UInt8}: Row dimensions of each block row.\ncolumnblocksizes::Vector{UInt8}: Column dimensions of each block column.\nm::Int: Total number of scalar rows.\nn::Int: Total number of scalar columns.\n\nExample\n\nusing BlockLinearSystems, SparseArrays\n\nsparsity_t = sparse([true false; true true])\nbsm = SparseBlockLinearSystem{Float64}(sparsity_t, [2, 3], [2, 3])\nblock(bsm, 1, 1) .= [1.0 2.0; 2.0 4.0]\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"BlockLinearSystems.SparseBlockLinearSystem"},{"category":"type","location":"api/#BlockLinearSystems.DenseBlockLinearSystem","page":"API Reference","text":"DenseBlockLinearSystem{T}(rowblockoffsets, columnblockoffsets=rowblockoffsets)\nDenseBlockLinearSystem{T}(data::AbstractMatrix{T}, rowblockoffsets, columnblockoffsets=rowblockoffsets)\nDenseBlockLinearSystem(data::AbstractMatrix{T}, rowblockoffsets, columnblockoffsets=rowblockoffsets)\n\nBlock-partitioned dense matrix backed by a matrix buffer data (such as Matrix{T}, SubArray, or FixedSizeMatrix{T}) alongside 1-based sentinel-terminated block offset vectors rowblockoffsets and columnblockoffsets.\n\nFields\n\ndata::AbstractMatrix{T}: Underlying matrix buffer of size (rowblockoffsets[end]-1, columnblockoffsets[end]-1).\nrowblockoffsets::Vector{Int}: 1-based start row of each block row, terminated by m + 1.\ncolumnblockoffsets::Vector{Int}: 1-based start column of each block column, terminated by n + 1.\n\nExample\n\nusing BlockLinearSystems\n\nbdm = DenseBlockLinearSystem{Float64}([1, 3, 6]) # Two blocks of sizes 2 and 3\nblock(bdm, 2, 1) .= reshape(1.0:6.0, 3, 2)\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"BlockLinearSystems.DenseBlockLinearSystem"},{"category":"type","location":"api/#BlockLinearSystems.TransposedPattern","page":"API Reference","text":"TransposedPattern(pattern::SparseMatrixCSC{Bool, Int})\n\nWrapper indicating that the provided block sparsity pattern is already in transposed orientation (size is (length(colblocksizes), length(rowblocksizes))).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"BlockLinearSystems.TransposedPattern"},{"category":"type","location":"api/#BlockLinearSystems.BlockOffsets","page":"API Reference","text":"BlockOffsets(offsets::Vector{Int})\n\nWrapper indicating that the provided vector represents 1-based start offsets of each block row/column, terminated by total_dimension + 1.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"BlockLinearSystems.BlockOffsets"},{"category":"function","location":"api/#BlockLinearSystems.block","page":"API Reference","text":"block(M, i, j)\nblock(M, i, j, rows, cols)\n\nReturns a mutable view into block (i, j) of M (SparseBlockLinearSystem or DenseBlockLinearSystem). When rows and/or cols are passed as StaticInt, returns a stack-wrapped SizedMatrix or HybridArray view for SIMD-accelerated block operations.\n\nExample\n\nusing BlockLinearSystems, Static\n\nbdm = DenseBlockLinearSystem{Float64}([1, 3, 5])\nB = block(bdm, 1, 1, static(2), static(2))\nB .= 1.0\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"BlockLinearSystems.block"},{"category":"function","location":"api/#BlockLinearSystems.blockrange","page":"API Reference","text":"blockrange(first, len) -> AbstractUnitRange\n\nIndex range of a block of len entries starting at index first. A StaticInt len gives a statically sized range; any other integer len gives a plain UnitRange{Int}, so views built with it are type stable and allocation free even when the size is only known at run time. Integer sizes of any type (including the UInt8 sizes stored by BlockRLE) are widened to Int before use.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"BlockLinearSystems.blockrange"},{"category":"function","location":"api/#BlockLinearSystems.validblock","page":"API Reference","text":"validblock(M, i, j) -> Bool\n\nReturns true if block (i, j) is structurally present in M (always true for DenseBlockLinearSystem, and M.indicestransposed[j, i] != 0 for SparseBlockLinearSystem).\n\nExample\n\nusing BlockLinearSystems, SparseArrays\n\nbsm = SparseBlockLinearSystem{Float64}(sparse([true false; true true]), [2, 2], [2, 2])\nvalidblock(bsm, 1, 2) # true\nvalidblock(bsm, 2, 1) # false\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"BlockLinearSystems.validblock"},{"category":"function","location":"api/#BlockLinearSystems.blocksizes","page":"API Reference","text":"blocksizes(sys, dim) -> Vector{UInt8}\nblocksizes(sys) -> Tuple{Vector{UInt8}, Vector{UInt8}}\n\nReturns the block dimensions along dimension dim (1 for rows, 2 for columns), or a tuple of (rowblocksizes, columnblocksizes).\n\nExample\n\nusing BlockLinearSystems\n\nsys = DenseBlockLinearSystem{Float64}([2, 3])\nblocksizes(sys, 1) # [2, 3]\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"BlockLinearSystems.blocksizes"},{"category":"function","location":"api/#BlockLinearSystems.nblocks","page":"API Reference","text":"nblocks(sys, dim) -> Int\nnblocks(sys) -> Tuple{Int, Int}\n\nReturns the number of blocks along dimension dim, or (n_rowblocks, n_colblocks).\n\nExample\n\nusing BlockLinearSystems\n\nsys = DenseBlockLinearSystem{Float64}([2, 3])\nnblocks(sys, 1) # 2\nnblocks(sys)    # (2, 2)\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"BlockLinearSystems.nblocks"},{"category":"function","location":"api/#BlockLinearSystems.blockdata","page":"API Reference","text":"blockdata(sys) -> Array\n\nReturns the underlying data storage buffer (a contiguous flat Vector{T} for SparseBlockLinearSystem or AbstractMatrix{T} for DenseBlockLinearSystem).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"BlockLinearSystems.blockdata"},{"category":"function","location":"api/#BlockLinearSystems.eachstoredblock","page":"API Reference","text":"eachstoredblock(sys)\n\nReturns an iterator yielding (i, j, block_view) for every structurally non-zero block in sys.\n\nExample\n\nusing BlockLinearSystems\n\nsys = DenseBlockLinearSystem{Float64}([2, 2])\nfor (i, j, B) in eachstoredblock(sys)\n    B .= 1.0\nend\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"BlockLinearSystems.eachstoredblock"},{"category":"function","location":"api/#BlockLinearSystems.zero!","page":"API Reference","text":"zero!(M)\n\nFills all stored entries of M (SparseBlockLinearSystem, DenseBlockLinearSystem, or AbstractArray) with zero in-place.\n\nExample\n\nusing BlockLinearSystems\n\nbdm = DenseBlockLinearSystem{Float64}([1, 3])\nbdm[1, 1] = 5.0\nzero!(bdm)\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"BlockLinearSystems.zero!"},{"category":"function","location":"api/#BlockLinearSystems.copy_structure","page":"API Reference","text":"copy_structure(M)\n\nCreates a new SparseBlockLinearSystem or DenseBlockLinearSystem sharing the block offset and sparsity index structures of M, with a freshly allocated zeroed data buffer.\n\nExample\n\nusing BlockLinearSystems\n\nbdm = DenseBlockLinearSystem{Float64}([1, 3, 5])\nbdm_copy = copy_structure(bdm)\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"BlockLinearSystems.copy_structure"},{"category":"function","location":"api/#BlockLinearSystems.uniformscaling!","page":"API Reference","text":"uniformscaling!(M, k)\n\nAdds scalar k to the main diagonal of square matrix M (SparseBlockLinearSystem, DenseBlockLinearSystem, or AbstractMatrix) in-place: M[i, i] += k.\n\nExample\n\nusing BlockLinearSystems\n\nbdm = DenseBlockLinearSystem{Float64}([1, 3, 5])\nuniformscaling!(bdm, 2.0)\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"BlockLinearSystems.uniformscaling!"},{"category":"function","location":"api/#BlockLinearSystems.symmetrifyfull","page":"API Reference","text":"symmetrifyfull(M) -> Matrix{T}\n\nConstructs a full symmetric Matrix{T} from a lower-triangular block matrix M (SparseBlockLinearSystem or DenseBlockLinearSystem). Allocates and returns a new symmetric Matrix{T}. See symmetrifyfull! for the in-place version.\n\nExample\n\nusing BlockLinearSystems\n\nbdm = DenseBlockLinearSystem{Float64}([1, 3, 5])\nbdm[3, 1] = 4.0\nmat = symmetrifyfull(bdm) # mat[1, 3] == 4.0, mat !== bdm.data\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"BlockLinearSystems.symmetrifyfull"},{"category":"function","location":"api/#BlockLinearSystems.symmetrifyfull!","page":"API Reference","text":"symmetrifyfull!(bdm::DenseBlockLinearSystem) -> AbstractMatrix{T}\nsymmetrifyfull!(mat::AbstractMatrix{T}, bsm::SparseBlockLinearSystem{T}, blocks=nothing)\n\nSymmetrifies a lower-triangular block matrix in-place:\n\nFor DenseBlockLinearSystem: mirrors the lower triangle to the upper triangle of bdm.data in-place and returns bdm.data.\nFor SparseBlockLinearSystem: populates preallocated dense matrix mat in-place. If blocks is a vector of block indices, extracts and symmetrifies only the principal submatrix corresponding to blocks.\n\nExample\n\nusing BlockLinearSystems, SparseArrays\n\nbsm = SparseBlockLinearSystem{Float64}(sparse([true true; false true]), [2, 2], [2, 2])\nblock(bsm, 2, 1) .= 3.0\nmat = zeros(4, 4)\nsymmetrifyfull!(mat, bsm)\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"BlockLinearSystems.symmetrifyfull!"},{"category":"function","location":"api/#BlockLinearSystems.symmetrifysparse","page":"API Reference","text":"symmetrifysparse(bsm::SparseBlockLinearSystem{T}) -> SparseMatrixCSC{T, Int}\n\nConverts a block-lower-triangular SparseBlockLinearSystem into a full symmetric SparseMatrixCSC{T, Int} by mirroring off-diagonal blocks to the upper triangle.\n\nExample\n\nusing BlockLinearSystems, SparseArrays\n\nbsm = SparseBlockLinearSystem{Float64}(sparse([true true; false true]), [2, 2], [2, 2])\nblock(bsm, 2, 1) .= 5.0\nS = symmetrifysparse(bsm)\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"BlockLinearSystems.symmetrifysparse"},{"category":"function","location":"api/#BlockLinearSystems.makesparseindices","page":"API Reference","text":"makesparseindices(bsm::SparseBlockLinearSystem, symmetrify::Bool=false) -> SparseMatrixCSC{Int, Int}\n\nPrecomputes a SparseMatrixCSC{Int, Int} whose nzval holds the 1-based indices into bsm.data for every scalar non-zero entry of sparse(bsm) (or symmetrifysparse(bsm) when symmetrify=true).\n\nThis enables zero-allocation copying from bsm.data into an existing SparseMatrixCSC via H.nzval[i] = bsm.data[sparseindices.nzval[i]].\n\nExample\n\nusing BlockLinearSystems, SparseArrays\n\nbsm = SparseBlockLinearSystem{Float64}(sparse([true true; false true]), [2, 2], [2, 2])\nidx_csc = makesparseindices(bsm, true)\nS = sparse(bsm, idx_csc)\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"BlockLinearSystems.makesparseindices"},{"category":"function","location":"api/#BlockLinearSystems.sparse_with_map","page":"API Reference","text":"sparse_with_map(sparse_sys::SparseBlockLinearSystem{T}; symmetric::Bool=false) -> (SparseMatrixCSC{T, Int}, Vector{Int})\nsparse_with_map(sparse_sys::SparseBlockLinearSystem{T}, sparse_idx::SparseMatrixCSC{Int, Int}) -> (SparseMatrixCSC{T, Int}, Vector{Int})\n\nPrecomputes a standard SparseMatrixCSC{T, Int} alongside a scatter_map::Vector{Int}. The scatter_map maps entries from sparse_sys.data to the CSC nonzeros vector.\n\nSubsequent updates can be performed in-place with zero allocations via:\n\ncopyto!(csc_mat, sparse_sys, scatter_map)\n\nExample\n\nusing BlockLinearSystems, SparseArrays\n\nsys = SparseBlockLinearSystem{Float64}(sparse([true true; true true]), [2, 2])\nblock(sys, 1, 1) .= 1.0\ncsc_mat, scatter_map = sparse_with_map(sys; symmetric=false)\n\n# In-place update in solver loop (0 allocations):\nblock(sys, 1, 1) .= 2.0\ncopyto!(csc_mat, sys, scatter_map)\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"BlockLinearSystems.sparse_with_map"},{"category":"function","location":"api/#BlockLinearSystems.block_sparse_nnz","page":"API Reference","text":"block_sparse_nnz(sparsity, blocksizes) -> Int\n\nComputes the total number of scalar non-zeros in a block-sparse matrix given its block sparsity::SparseMatrixCSC pattern and blocksizes vector.\n\nExample\n\nusing BlockLinearSystems, SparseArrays\n\nsparsity = sparse([true true; false true])\nblock_sparse_nnz(sparsity, [2, 3]) # 2*2 + 2*3 + 3*3 = 19\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"BlockLinearSystems.block_sparse_nnz"},{"category":"type","location":"api/#BlockLinearSystems.BlockRLE","page":"API Reference","text":"BlockRLE(runlengths, blocksizes)\nBlockRLE(runs::Vector{Pair})\nBlockRLE(vec::AbstractVector{<:Integer})\n\nRun-Length Encoded (RLE) block layout metadata capturing contiguous runs of uniform block sizes. Subtypes AbstractVector{Int} (storing the sizes compactly, normally as UInt8, but always returning Int) for transparent interop with all linear systems while providing O(1) matrix dimension lookup, O(1) run indexing, and O(log R) block-to-coordinate mapping.\n\nFields\n\nrunlengths::Vector{Int}: Number of blocks in each run.\nblocksizes::Vector{UInt8}: Block dimension in each run.\ncumblocks::Vector{Int}: 1-based start block index for each run, terminated by total_blocks + 1.\ncummatrixdims::Vector{Int}: 1-based start scalar matrix coordinate for each run, terminated by total_dimension + 1.\n\nExample\n\n# 100 camera poses (dim 6) followed by 50,000 landmarks (dim 3):\nrle = BlockRLE([100, 50_000], [6, 3])\nlength(rle)            # 50100\nmatrixdimension(rle)   # 150600\nnruns(rle)             # 2\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"BlockLinearSystems.BlockRLE"},{"category":"function","location":"api/#BlockLinearSystems.nruns","page":"API Reference","text":"nruns(rle::BlockRLE) -> Int\n\nReturns the number of uniform block runs in rle.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"BlockLinearSystems.nruns"},{"category":"function","location":"api/#BlockLinearSystems.runlength","page":"API Reference","text":"runlength(rle::BlockRLE, k::Integer) -> Int\n\nReturns the number of blocks in run k.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"BlockLinearSystems.runlength"},{"category":"function","location":"api/#BlockLinearSystems.runblocksize","page":"API Reference","text":"runblocksize(rle::BlockRLE, k::Integer) -> Int\n\nReturns the block dimension for blocks in run k.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"BlockLinearSystems.runblocksize"},{"category":"function","location":"api/#BlockLinearSystems.matrixdimension","page":"API Reference","text":"matrixdimension(rle::BlockRLE) -> Int\n\nReturns the total scalar matrix row/column dimension represented by rle (equivalent to sum(rle)).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"BlockLinearSystems.matrixdimension"},{"category":"function","location":"api/#BlockLinearSystems.runblockrange","page":"API Reference","text":"runblockrange(rle::BlockRLE, k::Integer) -> UnitRange{Int}\n\nReturns the 1-based range of block indices belonging to run k.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"BlockLinearSystems.runblockrange"},{"category":"function","location":"api/#BlockLinearSystems.runmatrixrange","page":"API Reference","text":"runmatrixrange(rle::BlockRLE, k::Integer) -> UnitRange{Int}\n\nReturns the 1-based range of scalar rows/columns in the represented matrix belonging to run k.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"BlockLinearSystems.runmatrixrange"},{"category":"function","location":"api/#BlockLinearSystems.eachrun","page":"API Reference","text":"eachrun(rle::BlockRLE)\n\nIterator yielding named tuples (run = k, blockrange = r, blocksize = s, matrixrange = m) for each uniform run in rle.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"BlockLinearSystems.eachrun"},{"category":"function","location":"api/#BlockLinearSystems.sort_blocks_hierarchical","page":"API Reference","text":"sort_blocks_hierarchical(roles::AbstractVector, dimensions::AbstractVector{<:Integer}; rev_dim::Bool=true)\n    -> (permutation::Vector{Int}, rle::BlockRLE)\n\nSorts blocks using a two-tier hierarchy (Role, Dimension) to cleanly separate Schur-eliminated variables from retained variables while grouping identical block dimensions into contiguous RLE runs.\n\nReturns the permutation vector permutation and the resulting BlockRLE metadata.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"BlockLinearSystems.sort_blocks_hierarchical"},{"category":"type","location":"api/#BlockLinearSystems.SchurStructure","page":"API Reference","text":"SchurStructure\n\nMetadata describing bipartite variable partitioning, block offsets, and coupling sparsity.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"BlockLinearSystems.SchurStructure"},{"category":"type","location":"api/#BlockLinearSystems.SchurBlockMatrix","page":"API Reference","text":"SchurBlockMatrix{T, BType}\n\nBlock Hessian structure partitioned into retained camera block B, eliminable landmark blocks C_data, coupling blocks E_data, and bipartite indexing structure::SchurStructure.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"BlockLinearSystems.SchurBlockMatrix"},{"category":"type","location":"api/#BlockLinearSystems.SchurReductionWorkspace","page":"API Reference","text":"SchurReductionWorkspace{T}\n\nPre-allocated scratch memory and caches for parallel Schur reduction and back-substitution.\n\nThe reduction runs in two phases. Phase 1 (parallel over chunks of eliminated blocks) factors every C_l, caching C_inv, z_l and W_cl = C_inv * E_cl' (W_data, laid out like E_data). Phase 2 (parallel over col_chunks, disjoint ranges of retained blocks) has each chunk write straight into the columns of S (and entries of g_S) that it owns, so no per-chunk copies of S are needed and nothing has to be merged afterwards.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"BlockLinearSystems.SchurReductionWorkspace"},{"category":"function","location":"api/#BlockLinearSystems.trial_workspace","page":"API Reference","text":"trial_workspace(ws)\n\nA workspace for a second linear system with the same structure: own C_inv/z caches and failure flag; the scratch (W_data, W_scratch) is only live within one solve and is shared.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"BlockLinearSystems.trial_workspace"},{"category":"function","location":"api/#BlockLinearSystems.reset_schur_dense!","page":"API Reference","text":"reset_schur_dense!(S, B, scaling_c, lambda, ws)\n\nReset dense reduced system S = B + lambda * diag(scaling_c) and the failure flag.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"BlockLinearSystems.reset_schur_dense!"},{"category":"function","location":"api/#BlockLinearSystems.reset_schur_sparse!","page":"API Reference","text":"reset_schur_sparse!(nzval, B_nzval, b_to_s_map, diag_inds, scaling_c, lambda, ws)\n\nReset sparse reduced system S = B + lambda * diag(scaling_c) and the failure flag.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"BlockLinearSystems.reset_schur_sparse!"},{"category":"function","location":"api/#BlockLinearSystems.reduce_schur_dense!","page":"API Reference","text":"reduce_schur_dense!(S, g_S, b_c, b_p, scaling_p, lambda, structure, C_data, E_data, ws)\n\nTwo-phase Schur reduction into the dense upper triangle of S (already holding B plus damping) and g_S.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"BlockLinearSystems.reduce_schur_dense!"},{"category":"function","location":"api/#BlockLinearSystems.reduce_schur_sparse!","page":"API Reference","text":"reduce_schur_sparse!(S, g_S, b_c, b_p, scaling_p, lambda, structure, C_data, E_data,\n                     block_colptr, block_rowval, block_row_offsets, col_heights, ws)\n\nTwo-phase Schur reduction into S.nzval (already holding B plus damping) and g_S.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"BlockLinearSystems.reduce_schur_sparse!"},{"category":"function","location":"api/#BlockLinearSystems.backsubstitute!","page":"API Reference","text":"backsubstitute!(x_p, x_c, structure, E_data, C_inv_data, z_data)\n\nRecover step Delta xp for all eliminable blocks independently using cached Cinv and z: x_p,l = z_l - C_inv,l * (sum_c E_cl' x_c,c). The coupling sum is accumulated first and C_inv is applied once per eliminable block.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"BlockLinearSystems.backsubstitute!"},{"category":"function","location":"api/#BlockLinearSystems.fast_bAb","page":"API Reference","text":"fast_bAb(H::SchurBlockMatrix, dx::AbstractVector) -> Real\n\nCompute quadratic form dx' * H * dx in O(N) operations without materializing the full Hessian. dx may have any element type; the result has the promoted type.\n\nThe retained part (B) is walked block by block. The eliminated part (C and the couplings E) is where nearly all the work is, and is processed per group of eliminated blocks of uniform dimension with that dimension known statically (3.4x faster than per-block run-time sizes on a 200-camera, 20000-landmark problem). Specialising the retained part by run of retained block sizes was measured too: it saved nothing measurable (that part takes ~0.01 ms) and cost 0.2-2 s of extra compilation, so it is not done.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"BlockLinearSystems.fast_bAb"},{"category":"function","location":"api/#BlockLinearSystems.build_schur_covisibility","page":"API Reference","text":"build_schur_covisibility(structure::SchurStructure, [T=Float64]; extra_blocks=()) -> NamedTuple\n\nCompute block-level and scalar-level co-visibility sparsity patterns for the reduced camera system S. Returns (block_covis, block_colptr, block_rowval, block_row_offsets, col_heights, S_sparse, diag_inds); S_sparse is a zero-valued SparseMatrixCSC{T}.\n\nThe pattern contains every pair of retained blocks that share an eliminated block, plus the diagonal. Retained-retained couplings that do not go through an eliminated block (e.g. odometry factors or loop closures between poses with no common landmark) have no such slot, so list them in extra_blocks, an iterable of (i, j) retained block index pairs (in either order; i == j is allowed and ignored). Use retained_block_pairs to obtain them from the block pattern of B.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"BlockLinearSystems.build_schur_covisibility"},{"category":"function","location":"api/#BlockLinearSystems.retained_block_pairs","page":"API Reference","text":"retained_block_pairs(B::SparseBlockLinearSystem) -> Vector{Tuple{Int, Int}}\n\nOff-diagonal stored blocks of B as (row_block, col_block) pairs. Pass them as extra_blocks to build_schur_covisibility so the reduced system has a slot for every coupling in B.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"BlockLinearSystems.retained_block_pairs"},{"category":"function","location":"api/#BlockLinearSystems.build_b_to_s_map","page":"API Reference","text":"build_b_to_s_map(B_sparse::SparseMatrixCSC, S_sparse::SparseMatrixCSC) -> Vector{Int}\n\nBuild mapping vector from Bsparse.nzval to Ssparse.nzval.\n\n\n\n\n\nbuild_b_to_s_map(B_sys::SparseBlockLinearSystem, S_sparse::SparseMatrixCSC, boffsets_c::Vector{Int}) -> Vector{Int}\n\nBuild mapping vector from Bsys.data to Ssparse.nzval for symmetric upper-triangle reduction. Entries of B_sys.data that are not mapped (the strict lower triangle of diagonal blocks) are 0.\n\n\n\n\n\nbuild_b_to_s_map(B_sys::SparseBlockLinearSystem, covis, boffsets_c::Vector{Int}) -> Vector{Int}\n\nAs above, but using the block-level pattern in covis (the result of build_schur_covisibility): one block-level search per stored block, then the scalar position follows from block_row_offsets and col_heights with integer arithmetic.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"BlockLinearSystems.build_b_to_s_map"},{"category":"function","location":"api/#BlockLinearSystems.sort_schur_blocks","page":"API Reference","text":"sort_schur_blocks(is_eliminable::AbstractVector{Bool}, block_sizes::AbstractVector{<:Integer}, observations::AbstractVector{<:AbstractVector{<:Integer}}) -> Vector{Int}\n\nTwo-pass variable ordering:\n\nPass 1: Retained variables (role 0) ordered by dimension descending; eliminable variables (role 1) grouped by dimension descending.\nPass 2: Within each eliminable dimension group, sort landmarks by primary observing camera index cmin(l) = min{c in obs(l)} c.\n\nReturns a block permutation vector mapping old block indices to new positions.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"BlockLinearSystems.sort_schur_blocks"},{"category":"function","location":"api/#BlockLinearSystems.expand_block_perm","page":"API Reference","text":"expand_block_perm(block_perm::AbstractVector{<:Integer}, block_sizes::AbstractVector{<:Integer}) -> Vector{Int}\n\nExpand a block-level permutation into a scalar-level permutation vector.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"BlockLinearSystems.expand_block_perm"},{"category":"function","location":"api/#BlockLinearSystems.computestartindices","page":"API Reference","text":"computestartindices(blocksizes) -> Vector{Int}\n\nComputes the 1-based starting scalar index for each block given a vector of blocksizes.\n\nExample\n\nusing BlockLinearSystems\n\ncomputestartindices([2, 3, 4]) # [1, 3, 6]\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"BlockLinearSystems.computestartindices"},{"category":"function","location":"api/#BlockLinearSystems.find_diag_inds","page":"API Reference","text":"find_diag_inds(A::SparseMatrixCSC) -> Vector{Int}\n\nFinds the index k in A.nzval corresponding to the diagonal entry A[col, col] for each column col in 1:size(A, 2).\n\nExample\n\nusing BlockLinearSystems, SparseArrays\n\nS = sparse([1, 2, 2], [1, 1, 2], [10.0, 20.0, 30.0], 2, 2)\nfind_diag_inds(S) # [1, 3]\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"BlockLinearSystems.find_diag_inds"},{"category":"function","location":"api/#BlockLinearSystems.inplacecumsum!","page":"API Reference","text":"inplacecumsum!(A::AbstractVector)\n\nSingle-argument in-place cumulative sum overwriting A with cumsum(A) and returning A.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"BlockLinearSystems.inplacecumsum!"},{"category":"function","location":"api/#BlockLinearSystems.factor_landmark!","page":"API Reference","text":"factor_landmark!(C_inv_data, z_data, W_data, scratch, structure, C_data, E_data, b_p, scaling_p, lambda, l, s_dp)\n\nPhase 1 for eliminated block l: invert C_l + lambda * diag(scaling), cache C_inv and z_l = C_inv * g_p,l, and store W_cl = C_inv * E_cl' for every observing retained block in W_data. Returns false if the damped block is numerically singular or not positive definite.\n\ns_dp is a StaticInt for blocks of up to D_STATIC dimensions (register arithmetic), otherwise the Int dimension, which uses a LAPACK Cholesky on the scratch matrix (O(d^3) per eliminated block plus O(d^2) per coupling row; no allocation).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"BlockLinearSystems.factor_landmark!"},{"category":"function","location":"api/#BlockLinearSystems.factor_landmarks_group!","page":"API Reference","text":"factor_landmarks_group!(ws..., p_chunk, s_dp) -> Bool\n\nPhase 1 over a chunk of eliminated blocks of uniform dimension. On failure the lowest failing eliminated block index is recorded in failed_landmark.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"BlockLinearSystems.factor_landmarks_group!"},{"category":"function","location":"api/#BlockLinearSystems.accumulate_dense_columns!","page":"API Reference","text":"accumulate_dense_columns!(S, g_S, structure, E_data, W_data, z_data, cam_ptr, cam_entry, entry_landmark, c_range)\n\nPhase 2 for the retained blocks in c_range: write all contributions to their columns of the dense upper triangle of S and to g_S directly. Chunks with disjoint c_range never write the same entry.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"BlockLinearSystems.accumulate_dense_columns!"},{"category":"function","location":"api/#BlockLinearSystems.accumulate_sparse_columns!","page":"API Reference","text":"accumulate_sparse_columns!(nzval, g_S, structure, E_data, W_data, z_data, cam_ptr, cam_entry, entry_landmark,\n                           S_colptr, block_colptr, block_rowval, block_row_offsets, col_heights, c_range)\n\nSparse counterpart of accumulate_dense_columns!, writing into S.nzval.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"BlockLinearSystems.accumulate_sparse_columns!"},{"category":"function","location":"api/#BlockLinearSystems.partition_columns","page":"API Reference","text":"Split the retained blocks 1:n into K contiguous ranges of roughly equal work (couplings plus the diagonal).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"BlockLinearSystems.partition_columns"},{"category":"section","location":"tutorials/symmetrification_and_schur/#Symmetrification-and-CSC-Scatter","page":"Symmetrification & CSC Scatter","text":"During Gauss-Newton or second-order optimization, only the block-lower triangle (i ge j) of the symmetric Hessian H needs to be accumulated. Once assembly is complete, the linear solver requires either a full symmetric Matrix{T}, a full symmetric SparseMatrixCSC{T, Int}, or a reduced Schur complement system.","title":"Symmetrification & CSC Scatter"},{"category":"section","location":"tutorials/symmetrification_and_schur/#Zero-Allocation-Scatter-into-SparseMatrixCSC","page":"Symmetrification & CSC Scatter","text":"Calling symmetrifysparse(bsm) constructs a new symmetric SparseMatrixCSC in a single call.\n\nIn iterative optimization loops where the sparsity pattern is fixed across iterations, precompute the solver matrix and scatter map once using sparse_with_map, and scatter bsm.data into H_csc with zero allocations in each step:\n\nusing BlockLinearSystems, SparseArrays\n\nsparsity = sparse([\n    true  false;\n    true  true\n])\nbsm = SparseBlockLinearSystem{Float64}(sparsity, [2, 2])\n\n# Precompute once during solver setup:\nH_csc, scatter_map = sparse_with_map(bsm; symmetric=true)\n\n# Inside each optimization iteration (0 allocations):\nzero!(bsm)\nblock(bsm, 1, 1, Val(2), Val(2)) .= [4.0 1.0; 1.0 5.0]\nblock(bsm, 2, 1, Val(2), Val(2)) .= [0.5 0.2; -0.1 0.3]\nblock(bsm, 2, 2, Val(2), Val(2)) .= [6.0 0.0; 0.0 7.0]\n\ncopyto!(H_csc, bsm, scatter_map)","title":"Zero-Allocation Scatter into SparseMatrixCSC"},{"category":"section","location":"tutorials/symmetrification_and_schur/#Extracting-Principal-Submatrices","page":"Symmetrification & CSC Scatter","text":"In bipartite problems such as Bundle Adjustment, variables partition into camera blocks C and landmark blocks L. symmetrifyfull! accepts an optional blocks index vector (or boolean mask) to extract and symmetrify only a selected subset of blocks into a preallocated dense matrix:\n\n# Extract only block 1 into a 2x2 dense matrix:\nH_sub = zeros(Float64, 2, 2)\nsymmetrifyfull!(H_sub, bsm, [1])\n\n# Or using a boolean mask:\nsymmetrifyfull!(H_sub, bsm, [true, false])","title":"Extracting Principal Submatrices"},{"category":"section","location":"tutorials/symmetrification_and_schur/#Choosing-Between-Sparse-and-Dense-Representations","page":"Symmetrification & CSC Scatter","text":"For small or densely connected graphs, dense Cholesky factorization outperforms sparse factorization due to lower indexing overhead and BLAS/SIMD efficiency. block_sparse_nnz computes the number of scalar non-zeros implied by a block sparsity pattern, which a solver can use to choose between a SparseBlockLinearSystem and a DenseBlockLinearSystem:\n\nblocksizes = [2, 2]\nnnz_lower = block_sparse_nnz(sparsity, blocksizes) # 2*2 + 2*2 + 2*2 = 12\n\nFor eliminating the landmark blocks rather than extracting the camera blocks, see the Schur reduction tutorial.","title":"Choosing Between Sparse and Dense Representations"},{"category":"section","location":"tutorials/block_assembly/#Block-Matrix-Assembly-and-Indexing","page":"Block Linear System Assembly & Indexing","text":"In factor-graph and nonlinear least squares problems, variables are naturally grouped into blocks mathbfx_1 dots mathbfx_K of dimensions d_1 dots d_K, and each cost factor contributes derivatives to a small subset of block pairs (i j).\n\nBlockLinearSystems.jl provides a unified interface across DenseBlockLinearSystem and SparseBlockLinearSystem so assembly code can remain agnostic to whether the underlying storage is dense or sparse.","title":"Block Matrix Assembly & Indexing"},{"category":"section","location":"tutorials/block_assembly/#Constructing-a-DenseBlockLinearSystem","page":"Block Linear System Assembly & Indexing","text":"A DenseBlockLinearSystem automatically allocates a full dense Matrix{T} partitioned into given block dimensions:\n\nusing BlockLinearSystems, Static, StaticArrays\n\n# Construct a block-square dense system with blocks of sizes 2, 3, 2\nbdm = DenseBlockLinearSystem{Float64}([2, 3, 2])\n\n# Or specify distinct row and column block sizes:\nbdm_rect = DenseBlockLinearSystem{Float64}([2, 3], [3, 2])\n\nIf you already have precomputed 1-based start offsets, you can pass the explicit offsets keyword or wrapper:\n\nbdm_offsets = DenseBlockLinearSystem{Float64}(; offsets=BlockOffsets([1, 3, 6, 8]))","title":"Constructing a DenseBlockLinearSystem"},{"category":"section","location":"tutorials/block_assembly/#Constructing-a-SparseBlockLinearSystem","page":"Block Linear System Assembly & Indexing","text":"A SparseBlockLinearSystem stores only the structurally non-zero blocks in a contiguous Vector{T}.\n\nYou can construct it using standard natural orientation sparsity patterns or coordinate lists:\n\nusing BlockLinearSystems, SparseArrays\n\n# Option A: Natural orientation lower-triangular pattern\nsparsity = sparse([\n    true  false false;\n    true  true  false;\n    false true  true\n])\nbsm = SparseBlockLinearSystem{Float64}(sparsity, [2, 3, 2])\n\n# Option B: Block coordinate list [(row, col), ...]\ncoords = [(1, 1), (2, 1), (2, 2), (3, 2), (3, 3)]\nbsm_coords = SparseBlockLinearSystem{Float64}(coords, [2, 3, 2])\n\n# Option C: Pre-transposed pattern (fast path for performance-critical pipelines)\nsparsity_t = TransposedPattern(sparse(sparsity'))\nbsm_fast = SparseBlockLinearSystem{Float64}(sparsity_t, [2, 3, 2])","title":"Constructing a SparseBlockLinearSystem"},{"category":"section","location":"tutorials/block_assembly/#Indexing-Blocks-with-block","page":"Block Linear System Assembly & Indexing","text":"Use block to obtain a mutable view into block (i j):\n\nDynamic dimensions: block(M, i, j) or block(M, i, j, rows, cols) returns a SubArray or ReshapedArray view.\nStatic dimensions: block(M, i, j, Val(R), Val(C)) or block(M, i, j, static(R), static(C)) returns a StaticArrays.SizedMatrix{R, C} view, allowing unrolled SIMD addition .+= with SMatrix{R, C}.\nHybrid dimensions: block(M, i, j, Val(R), cols) or block(M, i, j, rows, Val(C)) returns a HybridArrays.HybridArray view when one dimension is known at compile time and the other is dynamic.\n\nH_11 = @SMatrix [2.0 0.5; 0.5 3.0]\nblock(bsm, 1, 1, Val(2), Val(2)) .+= H_11","title":"Indexing Blocks with block"},{"category":"section","location":"tutorials/block_assembly/#Reusing-Structure-Across-Threads","page":"Block Linear System Assembly & Indexing","text":"When parallelizing Hessian assembly across threads, copy_structure creates a lightweight clone that shares the immutable index arrays (indices, indicestransposed, rowblocksizes, columnblocksizes) while allocating a separate data vector per thread:\n\nbsm_thread2 = copy_structure(bsm)\nzero!(bsm_thread2)\n\nAfter thread-local accumulation, the flat buffers can be reduced directly via bsm.data .+= bsm_thread2.data.","title":"Reusing Structure Across Threads"},{"category":"section","location":"#BlockLinearSystems.jl","page":"Home","text":"High-Performance Block-Dense and Block-Sparse Linear System Accumulators in Julia\n\nBlockLinearSystems.jl provides cache-friendly, zero-allocation block-structured matrix representations—SparseBlockLinearSystem and DenseBlockLinearSystem—designed for fast assembly of Gauss-Newton and second-order Hessians, factor-graph linear systems, and Schur complement solvers.","title":"BlockLinearSystems.jl"},{"category":"section","location":"#Key-Features","page":"Home","text":"Contiguous Block Storage (SparseBlockLinearSystem): Each non-zero matrix block (i j) is stored contiguously in column-major order within a single flat data::Vector{T} buffer. Accumulating a residual's local Hessian block into block(bsm, i, j) writes to contiguous memory without binary searches across compressed sparse columns.\nZero-Allocation Static Views: Indexing block(M, i, j, rows, cols) with StaticInt or Val dimensions returns stack-wrapped SizedMatrix or HybridArray views over the underlying storage for SIMD-accelerated block operations.\nZero-Allocation CSC Scatter: sparse_with_map precomputes a SparseMatrixCSC and an integer scatter map once during solver setup. In subsequent optimization iterations, copyto!(csc_mat, bsm, scatter_map) updates the sparse solver matrix in-place with zero allocations.\nDense & Submatrix Symmetrification: symmetrifyfull and symmetrifyfull! mirror lower-triangular block accumulators into full symmetric dense matrices or principal block submatrices (e.g. for Schur complement reduced camera systems).\nSchur Complement Reduction: SchurStructure, SchurBlockMatrix and the parallel reduce_schur_dense! / reduce_schur_sparse! kernels eliminate landmark blocks of any dimension (1–255) from bipartite systems, with dense or sparse reduced systems. See the tutorial.\nBlock Sparsity Statistics: block_sparse_nnz computes the scalar non-zero count implied by a block sparsity pattern, e.g. to help a solver choose between sparse and dense linear algebra.","title":"Key Features"},{"category":"section","location":"#Quick-Start","page":"Home","text":"using BlockLinearSystems, SparseArrays, Static, StaticArrays\n\n# 1. Construct a lower-triangular SparseBlockLinearSystem (blocks of size 2 and 3)\nsparsity = sparse([\n    true  false;\n    true  true\n])\nbsm = SparseBlockLinearSystem{Float64}(sparsity, [2, 3])\n\n# 2. Setup CSC matrix and scatter map for the solver\nH_sparse, scatter_map = sparse_with_map(bsm; symmetric=true)\n\n# 3. Accumulate into individual blocks using StaticInt or Val dimensions\nzero!(bsm)\nblock(bsm, 1, 1, Val(2), Val(2)) .+= @SMatrix [4.0 1.0; 1.0 5.0]\nblock(bsm, 2, 1, Val(3), Val(2)) .+= @SMatrix [0.5 0.0; 0.0 0.5; -0.5 0.5]\nblock(bsm, 2, 2, Val(3), Val(3)) .+= @SMatrix [3.0 0.0 0.0; 0.0 3.0 0.0; 0.0 0.0 3.0]\n\n# 4. In-place zero-allocation scatter into the solver's CSC matrix\ncopyto!(H_sparse, bsm, scatter_map)","title":"Quick Start"}]
}
